From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 12:07:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Paint z-index:auto positioned boxes in tree order

A fixed box is hoisted to the root for layout, and it was painted - and
hit-tested - from there too, after everything in body. CSS 2.1 Appendix
E paints z-index:auto positioned boxes of a stacking context in tree
order, so a later abspos box belongs on top of an earlier fixed one.

Base UI renders a transparent position: fixed backdrop just before the
menu positioner in the same portal. Painted from the root, the backdrop
sat above the menu and took every hit meant for it: a Base UI user menu
never saw the pointer reach its submenu item, so the submenu never
opened.

An auto-z fixed box now paints from its flow parent, the way a re-homed
abspos box already does. Paint only escapes the flow parent's own clip,
while a fixed box escapes every clip below its containing block, so a
box with a clipping ancestor further up keeps upstream's placement.
clips_descendants mirrors blitz-paint's clip decision for that check.

That check reads boxes above the flow parent, which can change without
damaging it. The containing block always rebuilds when one does, so it
re-checks its hoisted boxes and damages the chain down to any flow
parent whose decision flipped.

A viewport-fixed box does not scroll with the page, so
rehomed_paint_translate cancels the viewport scroll for it, and its
reach covers every scroll position: pure-scroll frames skip the resolve
that computes it.

rehomed_paint_translate maps a re-homed box from its containing block's
space into its paint parent's. The painter draws a paint parent's
re-homed children in its unscrolled space, so the paint parent's own
scroll is not subtracted (WPT position-fixed-scroll-nested-fixed,
position-sticky-escape-scroller-001 and -003,
hypothetical-box-scroll-parent). The walk also stops at an out-of-flow
paint parent: a hoisted box's location is relative to its containing
block, not its flow parent, so a fixed box under a fixed or abspos
ancestor would pick up the body margin (WPT
hypothetical-dynamic-change-001 and -002). Hit-testing descends in the
paint parent's scrolled content space, so it leaves that space for a
re-homed child explicitly, and the re-homed reach prune accepts the
point in either space.

A static box with no paint of its own (no background, border, shadow,
outline, text, clip or stacking context) whose paint children are all
positioned boxes contributes only to the positioned layer: CSS 2.1
Appendix E paints all of it at step 8, in tree order. Sorting it with
in-flow content put everything inside it under positioned siblings that
come earlier in the tree.

Popup libraries mount each popup into its own empty static portal div.
Base UI nests a submenu's portal inside the menu's, after the menu's
fixed backdrop, so the submenu painted and hit-tested under that
backdrop. The pointer "left" the submenu the moment it entered it, Base
UI closed the submenu, and "Light mode" was unreachable.

node_to_paint_order now ranks such a wrapper as positioned. The check
recurses through nested wrappers and is bounded, since portals are
shallow.
